.\" Man page for ha-agent.
.\"
.\" This source is uncompressed in the repository and compressed by the build:
.\" the .deb carries /usr/share/man/man1/ha-agent.1.gz, the .rpm carries the
.\" same file under %{_mandir}. Keep the environment names in step with the
.\" os.getenv() calls in ha-agent.py: the lifecycle tests in packaging/tests
.\" compare the two and fail on drift.
.TH HA-AGENT 1 "" "ha-agent" "HAProxy backend agent"
.SH NAME
ha-agent \- report backend weight and maintenance state to HAProxy's agent check
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ha-agent
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ha-agent
listens on a TCP port and answers each probe from HAProxy's
.BR agent-check
with the words that tell HAProxy how much traffic this backend should take, and
whether its operator wants it to take any.

The first word is a percentage: a share of the weight configured for the server
in HAProxy. A larger number therefore sends this backend more traffic, and
.B 0%
is acted on as a drain, since a weight of zero takes the server out of the load
balancing farm. The figure is built from measurements taken from
.IR procfs ,
each of them a fraction of headroom, and each weighted on its own: how much of
the CPU is idle, how much of it is not waiting on i/o, and how much memory is
available. A host is then tuned for what actually limits it, because i/o wait
matters more on a database server than available memory does. A fourth fraction,
how much of the recent sampling the busiest accelerator in the box was not busy,
joins that average when it is asked for with
.BR GPU_WEIGHT ;
it comes from
.I sysfs
and it is off by default, since not every host has a device that reports one. Sampling runs on
its own thread and a probe is answered from the sample on record, so polling by
several HAProxy instances does not itself register as load, and a reply is at
most
.IR REFRESH_INTERVAL
seconds old. Every connection the agent accepts is answered however fast they
arrive: a probe is never turned away, because an empty reply is an agent-check
failure, which costs whoever is polling more than waiting does.
.P
A probe does not have to send anything, and whatever it does send is read back
and discarded. After the reply the agent shuts down its own half of the
connection, so a reader sees where the reply ends, and it hangs up only once it
has taken the prober's bytes: closing while a peer's data sits unread makes the
kernel send a reset instead of a normal end, and a reset throws away the reply on
its way to the reader - which is what an empty answer from
.BR nc (1)
usually is.

Since every factor is headroom, an idle machine reports a high percentage and a
busy one a low one. They are averaged by the sum of their weights, so ordinary
CPU use moves the figure by tens of percent, and it only approaches
.B 0%
when available memory or i/o wait collapse - at which point the machine is not
one to add traffic to.
.P
The program runs in the foreground and writes its own diagnostics to standard
error; under
.BR systemd (1)
those lines reach the journal, tagged
.B ha-agent
by the unit's
.BR SyslogIdentifier .
It is not a syslog client and opens no syslog socket.
It shuts down cleanly on
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT ,
which is what
.BR systemd (1)
sends to stop it. It is normally started by the
.B ha-agent.service
unit installed alongside it.
.SH REPLY
The grammar is the one in the HAProxy configuration manual, section 5.2, under
.IR agent-check :
words separated by spaces, where a percentage sets the weight and
.BR ready ", " drain ", " maint
set the administrative state. The kinds of word are independent of one another,
which is what allows this agent to say nothing about something it does not know.
.TP
.B percentage
sent while load samples are being taken. When a sample cannot be taken - procfs
unreadable, a kernel reporting nonsense - the percentage is left out and nothing
else in the reply changes. HAProxy then keeps the weight its administrator
configured instead of following the last figure computed before the failure. The
journal says why, once, and says so again when sampling works.
.TP
.B "state word"
the first line of
.IR /etc/ha-agent.status ,
read on every pass, including the passes where the sample failed: a drained
backend stays drained even when the kernel side is unreadable. With nothing in
that file the agent sends no state word, so it does not undo a state that was
set on HAProxy's side by hand.
.TP
.B "never empty"
a reply with no bytes in it is an agent-check failure, and after
.I agent-fall
of those the server leaves rotation. So the reply always carries something: in
the first
.B REFRESH_INTERVAL
seconds after a start there is no sample yet, and if the state file says nothing
either, the reply is
.BR ready .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
There are no command line options. All configuration comes from the environment,
which
under
.BR systemd (1)
is read from
.I /etc/default/ha-agent
(Debian and Ubuntu) or
.I /etc/sysconfig/ha-agent
(RHEL and friends). Both paths are read, each one optional, so the same unit
file works on either family. Each line is
.BR "KEY=value" :
no shell expansion and no command substitution.
.P
Every value below is an integer. A value that is set but empty means the default
and the fallback is logged, so blanking a line is a way to unset it. A value that
is not an integer, or is outside its range, stops the agent at startup with one
line naming it;
.IB systemd
then retries on its restart schedule, so read the journal before assuming a
network problem.
.TP
.B PORT
TCP port to listen on. Default
.BR 7777 .
The service runs as the unprivileged user
.BR ha-agent ,
which cannot bind a port below 1024; if a low port is really wanted, give the
unit
.B AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
and a matching
.BR CapabilityBoundingSet .
.TP
.B HOST
Address to bind. Empty, the default, means
.B 0.0.0.0
- all interfaces, IPv4 only, which is why a prober that resolves
.I localhost
to
.B ::1
comes back refused on that attempt before it falls through to IPv4.
.B ::
binds all interfaces of both families with one socket, IPv4 clients arriving as
v4-mapped addresses, unless the system forces IPv6-only binds
.RI ( net.ipv6.bindv6only
set to 1). Any IPv6 address is accepted and the agent listens on the IPv6
family; a name is resolved by the system resolver in the IPv4 family, so a name
with only an AAAA record does not bind - write the address, or
.BR :: .
.TP
.B REFRESH_INTERVAL
Seconds between samples of CPU, i/o wait, memory and the accelerator when it is
switched on, and between re-reads of
.IR /etc/ha-agent.status .
Default
.BR 7 .
Neither the percentage nor the state word is fresher than this.
.TP
.BR CPU_WEIGHT ", " IOW_WEIGHT ", " MEM_WEIGHT
Relative weight of each factor in the reported percentage. Counts, not
fractions: 3 and 1 means the first counts three times what the second counts.
Default
.B 1
for all three, and at least one of them must be positive, because the three are
averaged by their sum. Raise the one that limits this host.
.TP
.BR GPU_WEIGHT
Weight of the accelerator factor, in the same counts-and-fractions sense as the
three above. Default
.BR 0 ,
which leaves the factor out and opens no
.I sysfs
path at all.
When it is positive, the factor is how much of the recent sampling the busiest
device in the box was
.I not
busy: the maximum across cards of
.IR /sys/class/drm/card*/device/gpu_busy_percent ,
which is the figure that device's own firmware computes for its activity level.
The agent names no vendor, no card and no device node, and reads nothing that
would need a driver's tool installed.

It is a ballpark for "do not send this new session here", which is what it was
built for, and not a measurement of the work: a device saturated by one large
batch and a device busy with forty small requests can report the same number.
Two limits follow from that and are worth reading before raising it. A host whose
driver exposes no such attribute has that factor left out of the average and is
scored on what the agent can see, with one line in the journal at startup saying
as much; and a device that exposes no percentage at all - an NPU, on the hardware
these boxes were tested on - is invisible, so it would be reported as idle, and
on a host that serves its work from one the honest setting is
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B GPU_SAMPLES
How many readings of that figure are averaged before it is used. Default
.BR 3 ,
which at the default
.I REFRESH_INTERVAL
is about 21 seconds of history. The reason for averaging rather than answering
from the newest reading is that the device reports a snapshot of something that
moves in seconds - it was measured going from 79% to 0% sixteen seconds apart
while serving inference - and the reply is a weight HAProxy redistributes traffic
on. A sample that cannot read the device at all clears the window rather than
smoothing across the gap, so the average is recent truth or nothing.
.TP
.B PROC_PREFIX
Where to read
.I procfs
from, default
.BR /proc .
Set it when the agent runs in a container with the host's
.I /proc
mounted elsewhere, for example
.IR /host/proc .
.TP
.B SYS_PREFIX
Where to read
.I sysfs
from, default
.BR /sys ,
opened only for the accelerator factor. Set it when the agent runs in a container
with the host's
.I /sys
mounted elsewhere, for example
.IR /host/sys .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/ha-agent
the program
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/ha-agent.service
the unit; enable it with
.BR "systemctl enable --now ha-agent" .
It runs the agent as an unprivileged user with the usual sandboxing on - no new
privileges, private
.BR /tmp ,
read-only system directories, restricted address families, namespaces and
personality. The agent opens nothing for writing, which is what makes that
list possible.
.TP
.I /etc/default/ha-agent
.I /etc/sysconfig/ha-agent
configuration, one of the two per host. Packaged as a configuration file, so an
upgrade keeps the copy on the host.
.TP
.I /etc/ha-agent.status
operator state, read once per
.BR REFRESH_INTERVAL .
Its first line is added to the reply, which is how a backend is taken out of
rotation locally without taking the machine down:
.IP
.EX
echo drain > /etc/ha-agent.status
.EE
.IP
A final newline is not required and blanks around the line are ignored, so a
file written with
.BR printf (1)
or by an editor that adds no newline works as written. Words inside the line are
sent as they stand, so anything
.I agent-check
understands may go there. An empty or missing file means no state word, which
leaves a state set on HAProxy's side untouched. Also packaged as a configuration
file, so an upgrade does not reset a drain.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
(for the
.B EnvironmentFile
syntax used by the configuration files),
the
.I agent-check
section of the HAProxy configuration manual,
https://github.com/avlis/ha-agent
